00:06:05.990 --> 00:06:10.960
- Yeah, is it fun though,
to be that person?

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:11.793
You know what I'm saying,
it's kind fun to play-

00:06:11.793 --> 00:06:13.693
- Well I was born for this part.

00:06:13.693 --> 00:06:14.526
(everyone laughing)

00:06:14.526 --> 00:06:15.570
- [Kelly] What does that mean?

00:06:15.570 --> 00:06:20.570
- It means that I'm that
older, white rich guy

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:22.898
who should not still be in the game.

00:06:22.898 --> 00:06:25.500
(everyone laughing)

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:27.180
- I have to typecast.

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:31.810
- Old white guys in a
position of power are like...

00:06:31.810 --> 00:06:33.800
- Yeah, yeah.

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:35.420
Wait, so you're passionate
about the environment.

00:06:35.420 --> 00:06:37.210
And I heard that you got into interested

00:06:37.210 --> 00:06:39.314
in cleaning up oceans,
pretty big time right?

00:06:39.314 --> 00:06:40.340
Is it back in the eighties?

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:42.900
- Yeah it was mostly coastal
pollution at that time

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:47.280
and offshore oil drilling and
trying to stop an oil company

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:49.650
from slant drilling into Santa Monica Bay.

00:06:49.650 --> 00:06:52.020
And my friend and I, Bob Sulnick,

00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:55.407
who was an environmental
lawyer naively kind of went,

00:06:55.407 --> 00:06:57.530
"Let's start an
environmental organization".

00:06:57.530 --> 00:07:01.430
And we did, and it was small,
but really well respected.

00:07:01.430 --> 00:07:03.760
People weren't talking about it that much.

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:07.440
We had an office in Washington and here,

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:09.720
and then that merged into Oceana,

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:13.140
which is now the world's
largest ocean advocacy group

00:07:13.140 --> 00:07:14.620
that focuses on oceans,
and it's brilliant.

00:07:14.620 --> 00:07:16.000
- Which is amazing, yeah.

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:17.899
- Check out oceana.org, but it's-

00:07:17.899 --> 00:07:22.277
- You should what you should
check out to scare you?

00:07:22.277 --> 00:07:27.277
To scare you, look at polluted waters.

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:30.580
There is an entire area of
the ocean that runs for miles,

00:07:30.580 --> 00:07:34.680
I feel like, with just trash
and that's just incredibly sad.

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:38.610
- Yeah, yeah it is sad and
terrible for the critters.

00:07:38.610 --> 00:07:42.180
It's also part of the whole
climate change because you drill

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:45.010
for oil, you make plastics,
you da da, da, da.

00:07:45.010 --> 00:07:47.300
So you're putting out all
these gases and the life-

00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:49.220
- But all that affects not
just them, it'll affect us,

00:07:49.220 --> 00:07:50.630
we're eating that fish.

00:07:50.630 --> 00:07:51.653
All of it affects all of us and-

00:07:51.653 --> 00:07:53.700
- Yes and it doesn't ever go away,

00:07:53.700 --> 00:07:55.970
but it gets small and smaller pieces.

00:07:55.970 --> 00:07:57.690
So yeah, it's not good.

00:07:57.690 --> 00:07:58.523
- It's just amazing to me

00:07:58.523 --> 00:08:01.220
'cause we can do much with
technology, we can do so much.

00:08:01.220 --> 00:08:02.090
We're talking about going to Mars

00:08:02.090 --> 00:08:04.570
but we can't just take
trash outta the ocean?

00:08:04.570 --> 00:08:05.490
- We're gonna get there.
(audience laughing)

00:08:05.490 --> 00:08:08.310
- Okay but isn't it kind of astonishing?

00:08:08.310 --> 00:08:10.050
Yeah, it's astonishing to me.

00:08:10.050 --> 00:08:13.130
- And it's usually for
a product that is made

00:08:13.130 --> 00:08:14.930
to be used once.

00:08:14.930 --> 00:08:15.763
- And then it's wasted and it's tossed.

00:08:15.763 --> 00:08:18.980
- And then it's there in
the environment forever.

00:08:18.980 --> 00:08:20.190
- [Kelly] Yeah.
- Not smart.

00:08:20.190 --> 00:08:22.430
So we wanna make sure
that you have a choice

00:08:22.430 --> 00:08:25.870
as a consumer when you
check out or you are dealing

00:08:25.870 --> 00:08:28.660
with Amazon, they should
give us all a choice.

00:08:28.660 --> 00:08:30.900
'Cause when you're given a
choice to do the right thing

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:31.733
for the environment, do it, they do it.

00:08:31.733 --> 00:08:33.280
- people choose the right thing, yeah.

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:35.800
But you gotta have the
option, I agree, I agree.

00:08:35.800 --> 00:08:39.940
Do you remember the first laugh
that you ever got on stage?

00:08:39.940 --> 00:08:41.390
- Yeah.
- [Kelly] You do?

00:08:41.390 --> 00:08:42.953
- Yeah.
- When was that?

00:08:42.953 --> 00:08:45.566
And did you fart like the
psychologist told you to?

00:08:45.566 --> 00:08:46.558
(everyone laughing)

00:08:46.558 --> 00:08:48.730
- No, I'm still very uptight, I was not.

00:08:51.750 --> 00:08:54.360
I wanted to be a basketball player.

00:08:54.360 --> 00:08:55.230
- [Kelly] That makes sense, you're tall.

00:08:55.230 --> 00:08:58.200
- Yeah and I was probably
a mediocre player,

00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:02.250
but our little school
won the championship.

00:09:02.250 --> 00:09:05.500
So my friend and I went to
Stanford and we both walked

00:09:05.500 --> 00:09:07.920
onto the freshman basketball court

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:09.750
to try out for the freshman team.

00:09:09.750 --> 00:09:11.910
And it was the same year that Lou Alshon

00:09:11.910 --> 00:09:15.400
or Kareem was a freshman at UCLA.

00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:18.350
And the game was, I was...

00:09:18.350 --> 00:09:20.810
I did what I'm doing now and turned around

00:09:20.810 --> 00:09:22.613
and walked off and had a good cry.

00:09:22.613 --> 00:09:23.446
(everyone laughing)

00:09:23.446 --> 00:09:24.410
- [Kelly] Oh no.
- Yeah.

00:09:24.410 --> 00:09:28.410
And then about a few
months later I was asking

00:09:28.410 --> 00:09:30.520
this girl out to have a cup of coffee.

00:09:30.520 --> 00:09:31.690
I finally had the nerve,

00:09:31.690 --> 00:09:34.540
her name was Beth and she said, "Okay".

00:09:34.540 --> 00:09:37.527
And we went and had coffee
and five minutes in she said,

00:09:37.527 --> 00:09:39.620
"I am so sorry, I have an audition

00:09:39.620 --> 00:09:40.900
I have to go to for a play".

00:09:40.900 --> 00:09:42.010
And I said, "Can I come?"

00:09:42.010 --> 00:09:43.610
Just wanting to be with her.

00:09:43.610 --> 00:09:46.190
And then to stay in the
room, I had to audition.

00:09:46.190 --> 00:09:50.590
So I made something up and
people laughed and I went-

00:09:50.590 --> 00:09:52.050
- You went up there with nothing?

00:09:52.050 --> 00:09:54.819
- Yeah.
- Whoa, you liked her a lot.

00:09:54.819 --> 00:09:56.480
(audience laughing)

00:09:56.480 --> 00:10:00.957
- Yeah, but I heard my
first laugh and it was like,

00:10:00.957 --> 00:10:04.510
"Okay it's not basketball,
but it's pretty good".

00:10:04.510 --> 00:10:05.930
- [Kelly] Felt good.
- Pretty, pretty good.

00:10:05.930 --> 00:10:07.590
- So one dream died in other was born.

00:10:07.590 --> 00:10:08.780
- Yep.
- See, there we go.

00:10:08.780 --> 00:10:10.070
I'm glad that you were bad at basketball,

00:10:10.070 --> 00:10:11.220
we would've missed out.

